{#- Multi-action commits (TYCHO_BATCH_ACTIONS). Included by actor.system.j2 only when enabled.
    Context vars: batch_max. Wording follows Franzen's Duck harness guard messages: the guards are a
    safety net, not a reason to stop batching. -#}
Committing several actions at once: take_action also accepts `actions`, a list such as [{"action":"ACTION4"},{"action":"ACTION4"},{"action":"ACTION6","row":12,"col":40}] (at most {{ batch_max }}). The harness executes them in order without asking you again, and stops early, skipping the rest, when the level completes, the game ends, an action changes nothing inside the board (the outer border, where timers and step counters usually sit, is ignored), the available actions change, or a validated plan's trajectory diverges. Your next turn then reports which actions ran, why the sequence stopped, and shows the board. Every action in a sequence still costs one scored action, but your turns are the scarce resource: one turn per action is too slow to get through the later levels. Use a sequence whenever you are confident of several steps: walking a route you can see, repeating a move until something changes, clicking a set of cells, or replaying a known solution or a validated plan. Commit single actions when the next outcome is genuinely uncertain, such as probing a mechanic for the first time. Before a sequence, locate the relevant objects in the current grid (e.g. with run_python) instead of counting earlier moves. When a sequence is stopped early, batching is still the right approach; the stop is what keeps a wrong movement model from costing the whole sequence.
